The danger: the pinned buffer's content is *behind* disk (disk has peers' renames), so naively forcing it to disk would clobber peers (lost update). Per Invariant #1, this node's own dir writes are drained-before-unlock — so this lingering pin shouldn't be its own pending rename. Let me examine the dir BAST drain/FUA path for a pin/bli leak.
Let me decode `bli_flags=0x2` and examine the BAST-release drain/evict ordering (lines 820-890).
`bli_flags=0x2` = `XFS_BLI_DIRTY`: this node has an **in-flight modification of the dir block that post-dates phase-2 `sync`**. That's the key — something re-dirties the dir block between sync and verify. Let me check the barrier mechanism (does it write into the test dir?).
Barriers live in a separate `.mxfs_barriers/` dir, so they don't re-dirty the test dir. Yet the test dir block is `BLI_DIRTY`+pinned at verify time. Let me capture exactly which assertions fail (own renames vs peers', name-missing vs content) to pin down the reader's view.
